        <h1 class="page-title">Employment Rights Guide for US Workers</h1>
        <p class="subtitle">What every employee should know about workplace protections</p>

        <p>Whether you're a full-time employee, part-time worker, or contractor, federal and state laws provide important protections in the workplace. Understanding these rights can help you recognize when an employer has crossed a legal line.</p>

        <h2>Wrongful Termination</h2>
        <p>Most US employment is "at-will," meaning employers can generally terminate you for any reason or no reason at all. However, there are important exceptions. It is illegal to fire someone for discriminatory reasons, in retaliation for reporting illegal activity, for taking legally protected leave, or for refusing to do something illegal.</p>

        <h2>Workplace Discrimination</h2>
        <p>Title VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964 prohibits discrimination based on race, color, religion, sex, and national origin. The Age Discrimination in Employment Act protects workers 40 and older. The Americans with Disabilities Act protects qualified individuals with disabilities. These protections generally apply to employers with 15 or more employees, though state laws often cover smaller businesses too.</p>

        <h2>Unpaid Wages and Overtime</h2>
        <p>The Fair Labor Standards Act (FLSA) sets the federal minimum wage and requires overtime pay at 1.5 times your regular rate for hours worked beyond 40 in a week, unless you are classified as an exempt employee. Many states have their own minimum wage rates that are higher than the federal rate, and employers must pay whichever rate is higher.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Keep your own records of hours worked if possible</li>
          <li>You generally have up to 2-3 years to file a wage claim depending on your state</li>
          <li>Retaliation for filing a wage complaint is illegal</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Workplace Harassment</h2>
        <p>Sexual harassment and other forms of harassment based on protected characteristics are illegal under federal law. This includes both "quid pro quo" harassment and hostile work environment situations. Most companies are required to have a process for reporting harassment, and you have the right to file a complaint with the Equal Employment Opportunity Commission (EEOC) if internal processes fail.</p>

        <h2>What to Do If Your Rights Are Violated</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Document everything — dates, times, witnesses, and what was said</li>
          <li>Report the issue through your company's HR process in writing</li>
          <li>File a complaint with the EEOC within 180 to 300 days depending on your state</li>
          <li>Consult an employment attorney, especially for serious violations</li>
          <li>Check your state labor department for additional state-specific protections</li>
        </ul>

